WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.719
Every tool you define on an MCP server creates a trust boundary,

2
00:00:04.719 --> 00:00:09.840
meaning a set of permissions the model can exercise on behalf of the user.

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.800
That framing changes how you approach tool design.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.520
Let me walk you through the least privilege principle,

5
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.120
access scoping mechanisms,

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.639
and input sanitization at the server boundary.

7
00:00:20.639 --> 00:00:25.579
Picture an MCP server that can reach every file on the host machine.

8
00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:29.200
That is the failure mode the rest of this lesson works to prevent.

9
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.240
Access scoping is the discipline that prevents it,

10
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.560
defining explicit boundaries on what data, systems, and operations

11
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.639
each server and its tools may reach, enforced through schema constraints,

12
00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:44.560
server-side authorization, and operating system permissions.

13
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.599
Least privilege narrows each tool to the permissions its specific function requires,

14
00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:54.319
and nothing more, which keeps the blast radius small when a tool is misused.

15
00:00:54.319 --> 00:01:00.080
Input sanitization validates and cleans every argument arriving at the server boundary

16
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.000
before it reaches any downstream system, which is where injection attacks get stopped.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.760
Why should you think of a tool definition as a trust boundary?

18
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.599
Because the schema and handler together define the full scope

19
00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:17.279
of what the model can do when it invokes that tool.

20
00:01:17.279 --> 00:01:20.239
The schema sets the expected argument types.

21
00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:23.279
The handler determines which systems the tool connects to

22
00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:25.519
and what operations it can perform.

23
00:01:25.519 --> 00:01:30.879
If you define a tool loosely, accepting freeform inputs and connecting to everything,

24
00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:34.720
you create a large and poorly controlled attack surface.

25
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.639
You've effectively handed the model and anyone who can influence its outputs

26
00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:40.480
a very broad set of permissions.

27
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.959
Treating every tool as a boundary forces you to ask, during design,

28
00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:50.160
exactly what should this tool be allowed to do and what should it explicitly not do?

29
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.919
That question, answered honestly for every tool,

30
00:01:53.919 --> 00:01:57.360
is how you build an MCP server you can actually defend.

31
00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:03.199
Applying least privilege to MCP tools starts with a simple rule,

32
00:02:03.199 --> 00:02:05.519
one tool per logical operation.

33
00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:09.600
A read-only search tool should have no write permissions at all.

34
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.119
A tool scoped to one database should have credentials that only reach that database.

35
00:02:15.119 --> 00:02:18.720
A file tool should be bound to specific directory paths,

36
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.679
rather than having access to the full file system.

37
00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:25.279
When you give each tool the minimum access it needs for its purpose,

38
00:02:25.279 --> 00:02:28.399
you shrink the blast radius of any misuse.

39
00:02:28.399 --> 00:02:32.639
If the model is manipulated into calling a tool in an unexpected way,

40
00:02:32.639 --> 00:02:35.839
or if the MCP server itself is compromised,

41
00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:38.559
narrow scope limits how much damage can result.

42
00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:41.839
Least privilege isn't just a security checkbox.

43
00:02:41.839 --> 00:02:46.320
It's a design discipline that makes the system easier to audit and reason about over time.

44
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.039
MCP gives you three concrete mechanisms to enforce access scoping.

45
00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:53.759
Schema constraints are the first layer.

46
00:02:53.759 --> 00:02:56.240
Declare a strict enum for an argument,

47
00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.119
and the server rejects inputs outside that set before your handler logic ever runs.

48
00:03:01.759 --> 00:03:04.320
Server-side authorization is the second.

49
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.880
Even after the schema validates the input,

50
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.320
your handler checks the caller's identity and permissions

51
00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.559
before touching any downstream resource.

52
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.360
Operating system permissions are the third.

53
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.000
File system and process limits,

54
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.039
on the host cap, what the server can actually reach.

55
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.440
It's worth clearing up roots here,

56
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.839
because people mistake them for enforcement.

57
00:03:25.839 --> 00:03:28.000
Roots are an advisory signal.

58
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.399
A client advertises the directories it wants a server to focus on,

59
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:34.720
and a well-behaved server should honor them,

60
00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.160
but nothing forces it to.

61
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.440
Real enforcement lives in those three layers.

62
00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:44.399
Broad scope versus narrow scope illustrates why this matters.

63
00:03:44.479 --> 00:03:48.960
A tool that accepts any SQL string and connects to any database

64
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.360
gives the model complete data access.

65
00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:54.639
An attacker who can influence the model's outputs,

66
00:03:54.639 --> 00:03:56.720
through prompt injection, for example,

67
00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.160
can potentially redirect tool calls to extract,

68
00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.479
modify, or delete arbitrary data through that one tool.

69
00:04:05.119 --> 00:04:08.240
A narrow design replaces that single permissive tool

70
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.240
with multiple specialized ones.

71
00:04:10.559 --> 00:04:12.479
One for reading a specific table,

72
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:14.800
with schema-constrained filter arguments.

73
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.480
Another for a different table with its own constraints.

74
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.079
Each tool connects with credentials, scoped to its purpose.

75
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.160
If any single tool is abused,

76
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.959
the damage is contained to what that tool can reach,

77
00:04:26.959 --> 00:04:28.480
which by design is very little.

78
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.880
Input sanitization happens at the server boundary,

79
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.760
and it should happen before any handler logic runs.

80
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.880
Every argument arriving at an MCP server

81
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.279
should be treated as untrusted input.

82
00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:43.839
Regardless of where the client claims it came from.

83
00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.200
The first step is type and format validation

84
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.880
against the declared schema.

85
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.279
If the schema says an argument is an integer,

86
00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:52.959
reject anything that isn't.

87
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.079
The next step is sanitizing or escaping characters

88
00:04:56.079 --> 00:04:59.200
that have special meaning in the downstream system.

89
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.760
SQL wildcards, shell metacharacters, path traversal sequences.

90
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.000
Length limits matter too.

91
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.760
An unbounded string argument can cause resource exhaustion,

92
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.440
or expose buffer overflow risks in downstream code.

93
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.640
Server-side sanitization is the last defense

94
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.359
before your tool handler reaches production systems.

95
00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:22.399
Designing tools with least privilege

96
00:05:22.399 --> 00:05:24.160
means making access decisions

97
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.559
before you write a single line of handler code.

98
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.079
Define one tool per logical operation,

99
00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:31.679
not one tool per domain.

100
00:05:32.239 --> 00:05:34.399
Use enum constraints wherever possible

101
00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:36.239
so the model can only select

102
00:05:36.239 --> 00:05:38.880
from a valid pre-approved set of values.

103
00:05:40.079 --> 00:05:42.559
Filter the results your tool returns

104
00:05:42.559 --> 00:05:46.079
to include only what the caller is actually authorized to see.

105
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.720
Don't return raw database rows

106
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.799
and hope the host application filters them.

107
00:05:51.359 --> 00:05:54.160
Tools designed this way are narrow,

108
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.920
auditable, and safe by default.

109
00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.799
Tools designed without this discipline

110
00:05:58.799 --> 00:06:01.119
may work correctly in normal use,

111
00:06:01.119 --> 00:06:03.760
but they're brittle under adversarial conditions

112
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.920
and hard to secure after the fact.

113
00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.119
A few scoping mistakes come up repeatedly

114
00:06:09.119 --> 00:06:11.040
in MCP server designs.

115
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.679
The first is overly permissive schemas.

116
00:06:13.679 --> 00:06:15.920
Using free-form string arguments

117
00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.519
where an enum would work

118
00:06:17.519 --> 00:06:21.119
allows the model to craft arbitrary downstream queries.

119
00:06:21.119 --> 00:06:23.200
The second is shared credentials.

120
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.640
When all tools on a server

121
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.959
share one set of backend credentials,

122
00:06:26.959 --> 00:06:30.559
any tool's compromise exposes every system

123
00:06:30.559 --> 00:06:32.239
those credentials can reach.

124
00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:35.200
The third is missing output filtering.

125
00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:38.559
Returning raw query results without filtering

126
00:06:38.559 --> 00:06:42.079
can expose records the calling user has no right to see.

127
00:06:42.079 --> 00:06:44.239
Each of these is a design decision

128
00:06:44.239 --> 00:06:46.959
that feels harmless in a development environment,

129
00:06:46.959 --> 00:06:49.920
but creates significant exposure in production.

130
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.279
Fixing them early during tool design

131
00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:56.239
is much cheaper than auditing and remediating them later.

132
00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.000
When a tool's scope is too wide,

133
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.519
you don't see it fail.

134
00:07:01.519 --> 00:07:02.880
You see it work.

135
00:07:02.959 --> 00:07:05.200
On data, it was never meant to reach.

136
00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.440
That's the uncomfortable part.

137
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.160
A tool definition is a grant of permission,

138
00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.640
and the model will use every bit of reach

139
00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:13.600
that you give to it.

140
00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:15.600
Narrow the grant,

141
00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.279
and one compromised tool stays one compromised tool

142
00:07:19.279 --> 00:07:21.279
instead of compromising the entire system.

143
00:07:22.079 --> 00:07:23.760
That closes out this lesson.

144
00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:24.959
I'll see you in the next one.

